    "confidence": 0.7,
    "explanation": "Rider and Driver — the route facts are agreed, and the case turns on one element the record cannot carry, so it goes to a human reviewer.\n\nDeviation and traffic. On the platform's recorded distances, which POL-RD-22 makes the measure, the deviation is 13.2 km minus 12.0 km = 1.20 km, over the 1.0 km tolerance. The GPS trace shows which road was taken and when and is not used to re-measure the distance — its 11.12 km does not shrink the deviation (POL-RD-22). The reroute was traffic-justified: the LTA advisory records an ACCIDENT on the Central Expressway (CTE) towards SLE after the Braddell Road exit, valid 20:23–21:40, which sits on the CTE — the expressway the recommended route uses — and covers the deviation event at 20:54:05 (POL-RD-23). Driver, your POL-RD-23 point is accepted; Rider, I reject the argument that this advisory fails POL-RD-23, because its own road, direction and location put it on the segment the driver avoided. The driver's further point that the diversion was brief and still reached the booked drop-off is noted but does not bear on the matrix.\n\nNotice — the element the matrix turns on, and one the record cannot establish. POL-RD-24 asks whether the rider was informed before or at the point of deviation. POL-RD-21 requires notice by in-app chat, or verbally with the trip call recording as evidence. The chat log carries no notice of the deviation: its only two messages (20:38:52, 20:39:12) precede the 20:40:02 trip start and concern the pickup point ('At Dempsey Block 8 drop off' / 'ok'), and trip_record.in_app_calls is empty. Driver, your account is that you told the passenger verbally and he nodded; Rider, yours is that you were never told. POL-RD-21 provides that a verbal notice with no recording in the record cannot be established, and POL-GEN-26 sends a fact the record cannot carry — whether the driver notified the rider — to a human reviewer. Rider, your claim that the record shows you were not informed is rejected: a verbal claim the policy cannot test leaves the question open rather than answered, so row (b) of POL-RD-24 is no more made out than row (a). Rows (c) and (e) are excluded by the 1.20 km deviation, and rows (a) and (b) both need the missing notice element; POL-RD-24 sends a case that fits no row to escalation. POL-RD-25 holds an AI ruling on a route deviation to a confidence of 0.80, which this element cannot reach — so Rider, your claim that the case is decidable at that confidence is also rejected.\n\nMoney and referrals. The sum in issue is the excess distance fare: 1.20 km times 0.72 SGD/km = 0.86 SGD, the amount already refunded to the rider and recovered from the driver's earnings (POL-RD-24). Because the case is escalated, no money moves: that 0.86 SGD is held pending the reviewer's decision (POL-GEN-03). Neither profile carries a fraud flag, so no 0.95 uplift applies, and the contested 0.86 SGD sits in POL-GEN-22's below-10.00 SGD band — which is why the bar is POL-RD-25's 0.80. No clause adds a referral."
